  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">CO-ERF for local actors in Southwest Uga</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The allocated humanitarian funding in Uganda serves as a lifeline, facilitating the provision offundamental services such as shelter, sustenance, clean and safe drinking water, and healthcare torefugees and marginalized groups of people. Moreover, these funds enable the implementation ofvital programs aimed at empowering refugees and supporting host communities to overcome theeffects of shocks and leave dignified life. These programs encompass initiatives designed to bolsterlivelihoods, expand educational opportunities, and ensure access to legal protection for those inneed.It should also be recall that, Oxfam is a signatory to the Grand Bargain Commitments as well asthe Charter for Change that advocates for meaningful participation in humanitarian preparedness,implementation and coordination, and investments in the leadership of local and nationalhumanitarian actors under the localization debates and practices.In the past, Oxfam has been actively engaged in supporting local and national actors throughinitiatives such as the Empowering Local and National Humanitarian Actors (ELNHA) Project,encompassing both Phase I and II. These projects, initiated in 2016, effectivelycarried outinterventions in Arua, Koboko, Lamwo, and Kodito districts. The final evaluation of the projectemphasized the importanceof advocating for the establishment of new national pooled fundingmechanisms to strengthen localization efforts and increase investments to local and nationalhumanitarian actors for timely and effective response to disasters of emergency nature.In line with this recommendations, Oxfam in Uganda oversaw the initial phase of the EmergencyResponse Fund (ERF), with support from Oxfam America. Thefund was hosted by World VoicesUganda, a national NGO operating in Western Uganda. This fund was efficiently managed by sixlocal actors associated with the Western Uganda Humanitarian Platform, working in Isingiro andKyegegwa districts, where refugees are currently residing in settlements. Moreover, the fundplayed a crucial role in addressing urgent issues concerning flooding and land displacement inKasese, Bundibugyo, and Kikuube districts. It facilitated a swift response to various challenges,including floods and food shortages, by providing essential food and non-food items to thoseaffected by disasters. Approximately 80,000 individuals received assistance through this initiative</narrative>
  </description>
